1
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:06,760
A shop called Northstar lost two
point four million dollars because of

2
00:00:06,780 --> 00:00:10,404
one customer who was deleted,
but not really gone.

3
00:00:10,495 --> 00:00:13,631
Every dashboard said the
deletion was complete.

4
00:00:14,242 --> 00:00:17,415
Weeks later, the same person
tried to sign up again,

5
00:00:17,435 --> 00:00:21,270
and the system said: this
email is already registered.

6
00:00:21,290 --> 00:00:25,308
The account was supposed to be gone,
so where did that email come from?

7
00:00:25,733 --> 00:00:29,022
Their data was still sitting
in a database replica,

8
00:00:29,042 --> 00:00:32,400
a search index, support
attachments, exports,

9
00:00:32,420 --> 00:00:35,476
analytics, logs and backups.

10
00:00:35,636 --> 00:00:39,262
And a sync job had even
re-created some of it.

11
00:00:39,282 --> 00:00:42,906
So the word complete was
a claim, not a fact.

12
00:00:43,610 --> 00:00:45,285
Think of a building inspector.

13
00:00:45,305 --> 00:00:47,444
The builder says the job is done.

14
00:00:47,464 --> 00:00:51,750
The inspector walks into every
room and checks for themselves.

15
00:00:52,360 --> 00:00:57,389
Our job is to build an auditor that
does not trust the word complete.

16
00:00:57,480 --> 00:01:01,187
For each deletion request, it
checks what is really left,

17
00:01:01,207 --> 00:01:05,042
decides complete, incomplete,
or not applicable,

18
00:01:05,062 --> 00:01:07,502
and lists the steps to fix it.

19
00:01:07,662 --> 00:01:10,510
Getting this wrong costs money and trust,

20
00:01:10,530 --> 00:01:14,084
so the auditor must show its
evidence for every step.

21
00:01:17,734 --> 00:01:20,942
Everything we get is in the dataset folder.

22
00:01:20,962 --> 00:01:26,050
There are 518 requests to audit, and
32 sample requests with the correct

23
00:01:26,070 --> 00:01:27,072
answers.

24
00:01:27,092 --> 00:01:30,810
There are 850 customers
with their current details,

25
00:01:30,830 --> 00:01:34,396
and a history file of
changed emails and phones.

26
00:01:34,487 --> 00:01:38,833
An activity log holds about four thousand
events from the deletion worker,

27
00:01:38,853 --> 00:01:41,408
syncs, backups and exports.

28
00:01:41,569 --> 00:01:44,591
A rules table has 15 deletion rules.

29
00:01:44,611 --> 00:01:48,701
And there are 94 evidence
files: database snapshots,

30
00:01:48,721 --> 00:01:52,879
logs, JSON exports, PDFs and screenshots.

31
00:01:53,040 --> 00:01:56,271
The evidence files do not
say whose data they hold.

32
00:01:56,291 --> 00:01:58,999
We have to connect them
through emails, phones,

33
00:01:59,019 --> 00:02:01,076
hashes and record ids.

34
00:02:01,687 --> 00:02:06,287
Each request has an email, which
may be a current or an old address,

35
00:02:06,307 --> 00:02:09,131
the time it was made,
what the workflow claims:

36
00:02:09,151 --> 00:02:12,347
complete, partially
complete, or not started,

37
00:02:12,367 --> 00:02:15,957
and the audit time: the
moment we must describe.

38
00:02:16,117 --> 00:02:19,950
Anything that happened after
the audit time does not count.

39
00:02:20,654 --> 00:02:24,570
We hand in one row per request: the case id,

40
00:02:24,590 --> 00:02:27,645
a status, and an ordered list of steps.

41
00:02:27,736 --> 00:02:30,989
Each step is a finding, a fix, or a check.

42
00:02:31,150 --> 00:02:34,450
Findings come first,
then fixes, then checks,

43
00:02:34,470 --> 00:02:38,489
and every finding must cite the
evidence file that proves it.

44
00:02:42,139 --> 00:02:46,402
The rules table decides what must
happen to each kind of record.

45
00:02:46,562 --> 00:02:50,478
Delete: the profile, search
entries, cache entries,

46
00:02:50,498 --> 00:02:55,169
user exports, marketing profiles,
and attachments that belong only to

47
00:02:55,189 --> 00:02:56,421
this person.

48
00:02:57,125 --> 00:03:01,830
Redact means keep the record,
but remove names and emails.

49
00:03:01,850 --> 00:03:05,532
That applies to shared support
messages and email logs.

50
00:03:05,565 --> 00:03:08,656
Anonymize means cut the link to the person.

51
00:03:08,676 --> 00:03:11,697
That applies to analytics and telemetry.

52
00:03:12,238 --> 00:03:17,106
Retain covers security audit logs
inside their retention period,

53
00:03:17,126 --> 00:03:21,865
legal holds, and backups, as long
as restoring them can be blocked.

54
00:03:22,290 --> 00:03:25,333
And one special case: the tombstone.

55
00:03:25,424 --> 00:03:29,212
A deleted account may keep
only its internal user id,

56
00:03:29,232 --> 00:03:31,858
so other records still point somewhere.

57
00:03:31,949 --> 00:03:36,084
The name, email, phone and
email hash are removed.

58
00:03:36,300 --> 00:03:38,521
So, complete means three things.

59
00:03:38,541 --> 00:03:40,576
Every copy got the right treatment.

60
00:03:40,596 --> 00:03:42,677
Nothing can re-create the data.

61
00:03:42,697 --> 00:03:45,450
And what must be kept is still there.

62
00:03:49,100 --> 00:03:51,842
Our solution works in six steps.

63
00:03:51,991 --> 00:03:55,835
Step one: is this a deletion request at all?

64
00:03:55,903 --> 00:04:01,549
Ten requests, cases 533 to 542, ask
for things like a new phone number,

65
00:04:01,569 --> 00:04:03,778
or no more promotional emails.

66
00:04:03,798 --> 00:04:06,993
They get not applicable, with no steps.

67
00:04:07,130 --> 00:04:09,849
Step two: who is this person?

68
00:04:09,929 --> 00:04:14,227
The request email may be an old one,
so we use the history file to collect

69
00:04:14,247 --> 00:04:17,988
every email, phone and hash they ever had.

70
00:04:18,148 --> 00:04:23,144
Step three: read every evidence
file into one common format:

71
00:04:23,164 --> 00:04:26,790
this resource, at this time,
with these identifiers,

72
00:04:26,810 --> 00:04:28,309
in this state.

73
00:04:28,470 --> 00:04:32,675
Step four: decide which
resources belong to the person.

74
00:04:32,708 --> 00:04:36,264
Step five: work out the
state at the audit time,

75
00:04:36,284 --> 00:04:40,546
using only evidence captured
by then, and apply the rules.

76
00:04:40,637 --> 00:04:44,623
Step six: write the findings,
the fixes and the checks,

77
00:04:44,643 --> 00:04:46,479
in the required order.

78
00:04:47,020 --> 00:04:51,285
The key design choice: ordinary
code does steps two to six,

79
00:04:51,305 --> 00:04:53,570
so results are repeatable.

80
00:04:53,661 --> 00:04:57,240
The language model does
one thing only: step one,

81
00:04:57,260 --> 00:05:00,165
reading a request in any language.

82
00:05:03,813 --> 00:05:08,437
Some resources carry no email anywhere:
an account that simply returns

83
00:05:08,457 --> 00:05:12,895
not found, or an analytics row
that has already been anonymized.

84
00:05:12,915 --> 00:05:14,716
We still must report them.

85
00:05:15,142 --> 00:05:18,673
The only clue is the deletion
worker's activity log.

86
00:05:18,764 --> 00:05:23,805
Each case leaves a small cluster of rows
near the time the workflow reported

87
00:05:23,825 --> 00:05:25,116
its result.

88
00:05:25,149 --> 00:05:28,844
And the result in the log usually
matches the reported status:

89
00:05:28,864 --> 00:05:31,606
success, partial, or failed.

90
00:05:32,217 --> 00:05:35,192
When several cases share
the same report time,

91
00:05:35,212 --> 00:05:39,941
we narrow it down by elimination,
and by a timing fingerprint:

92
00:05:39,961 --> 00:05:44,167
rows of one case keep the same offset
in seconds from that case's account

93
00:05:44,187 --> 00:05:45,013
row.

94
00:05:45,162 --> 00:05:47,998
We measure that offset from the data itself,

95
00:05:48,018 --> 00:05:49,819
never from the answer key.

96
00:05:50,302 --> 00:05:54,961
In the samples, cases 25
and 26 share a report time,

97
00:05:54,981 --> 00:05:58,164
and so do cases 27 and 28.

98
00:05:58,255 --> 00:06:05,224
Accounts 3136 and 5684, and
accounts 3866 and 5482,

99
00:06:05,244 --> 00:06:07,034
are told apart this way.

100
00:06:07,195 --> 00:06:12,432
It is a strong clue, not a proof, which
is why we list it as a limit later.

101
00:06:16,083 --> 00:06:19,568
Here is one worked example: case 5.

102
00:06:19,659 --> 00:06:24,353
On 27 August, Leila Haddad asked us
to remove any remaining copy of her

103
00:06:24,373 --> 00:06:25,477
profile.

104
00:06:25,499 --> 00:06:29,020
The workflow said complete on 28 August.

105
00:06:29,040 --> 00:06:31,816
The audit time is 8 September.

106
00:06:32,299 --> 00:06:36,900
Evidence 90, a snapshot of the
account service from 5 September,

107
00:06:36,920 --> 00:06:38,930
lists no account for her.

108
00:06:39,091 --> 00:06:41,032
A naive check says done.

109
00:06:41,065 --> 00:06:46,385
But evidence 54, a failover replica
captured on 2 September at eleven

110
00:06:46,405 --> 00:06:50,298
at night, still holds
account zero zero five,

111
00:06:50,318 --> 00:06:53,013
with her name, email and phone.

112
00:06:53,624 --> 00:06:57,191
A snapshot only proves
absence for its own database,

113
00:06:57,211 --> 00:06:58,759
not for every copy.

114
00:06:58,779 --> 00:07:02,577
So the account is still present,
and the case is incomplete.

115
00:07:03,060 --> 00:07:05,002
The output has three steps.

116
00:07:05,022 --> 00:07:09,426
A finding: report remaining
record, account zero zero five,

117
00:07:09,446 --> 00:07:11,689
citing evidence 54.

118
00:07:11,722 --> 00:07:13,791
A fix: delete the record.

119
00:07:13,811 --> 00:07:16,740
A check: verify the record is absent.

120
00:07:16,760 --> 00:07:19,689
The fix and the check carry no evidence id,

121
00:07:19,709 --> 00:07:22,288
because they describe future work.

122
00:07:25,938 --> 00:07:27,149
Now the results.

123
00:07:27,169 --> 00:07:31,468
On the 32 public samples:
status accuracy is 100 percent,

124
00:07:31,488 --> 00:07:36,100
step precision 100 percent,
step recall 98.4 percent,

125
00:07:36,120 --> 00:07:39,676
and 29 of 32 cases match the answer exactly,

126
00:07:39,696 --> 00:07:40,789
in order.

127
00:07:41,202 --> 00:07:48,031
The three misses are account 004,
and audit records 6645 and 5569.

128
00:07:48,099 --> 00:07:50,993
None of them appears anywhere
in the participant data,

129
00:07:51,013 --> 00:07:53,360
so we do not guess ids.

130
00:07:53,739 --> 00:07:57,352
There is no answer key
for the 518 real cases,

131
00:07:57,372 --> 00:08:01,461
so we claim consistency with
the spec, not a percentage.

132
00:08:01,622 --> 00:08:04,748
The full output passes
a validator for format,

133
00:08:04,768 --> 00:08:09,067
for order, and for the rule that no
evidence newer than the audit time

134
00:08:09,087 --> 00:08:10,320
is cited.

135
00:08:10,931 --> 00:08:12,999
Now, the mistake we caught.

136
00:08:13,160 --> 00:08:19,291
Our first model-assisted run dropped
sample status accuracy to 68.8 percent.

137
00:08:19,452 --> 00:08:25,040
GPT-4o mini labelled real requests,
such as asking us to verify an account

138
00:08:25,060 --> 00:08:30,054
is gone, as not deletion requests,
and that emptied those cases.

139
00:08:30,215 --> 00:08:34,827
We rewrote the prompt, made the model
unable to veto without an explicit

140
00:08:34,847 --> 00:08:38,308
decline, and added a deterministic guard.

141
00:08:38,469 --> 00:08:44,903
Even a model that rejects everything
now yields exactly cases 533 to 542.

142
00:08:45,328 --> 00:08:51,728
The final run: 375 model
calls, 146,396 tokens,

143
00:08:51,748 --> 00:08:56,974
about 2.7 cents, and output
identical to the no-model run.

144
00:09:00,624 --> 00:09:02,448
First, the limits.

145
00:09:02,482 --> 00:09:05,770
Records that appear nowhere
cannot be reported.

146
00:09:05,790 --> 00:09:09,846
The timing match for records without
identifiers can pair wrongly when

147
00:09:09,866 --> 00:09:12,247
cases share a report time.

148
00:09:12,338 --> 00:09:15,523
The model path was only
checked against the samples.

149
00:09:15,543 --> 00:09:18,691
And the vision fallback is untested.

150
00:09:19,302 --> 00:09:23,461
Question one: how do you know
it works with no answer key?

151
00:09:23,481 --> 00:09:26,991
The samples, four hand-built
cases, a validator,

152
00:09:27,011 --> 00:09:30,021
a second no-model run
that must be identical,

153
00:09:30,041 --> 00:09:32,260
and independent testers.

154
00:09:32,351 --> 00:09:35,326
We never claim a percentage on the 518.

155
00:09:35,809 --> 00:09:38,817
Question two: what does the model do?

156
00:09:38,851 --> 00:09:42,358
It only decides whether a
request asks for deletion.

157
00:09:42,392 --> 00:09:47,455
Everything else is code, so the
output is repeatable and costs cents.

158
00:09:48,066 --> 00:09:52,272
Question three: why not let the
model read all the evidence?

159
00:09:52,292 --> 00:09:57,206
Cost, repeatability, and it is easy for
a model to get ordering and evidence

160
00:09:57,226 --> 00:09:58,656
ids wrong.

161
00:09:58,689 --> 00:10:00,397
Code can be tested.

162
00:10:00,823 --> 00:10:04,378
Question four: how do you
handle replica drift?

163
00:10:04,398 --> 00:10:06,397
State is tracked per store.

164
00:10:06,430 --> 00:10:10,414
Any store still holding the
record keeps it non-compliant.

165
00:10:10,955 --> 00:10:14,511
Question five: what about
evidence from the future?

166
00:10:14,531 --> 00:10:18,412
Anything captured after the audit
time is never used for state,

167
00:10:18,432 --> 00:10:19,919
and never cited.

168
00:10:20,461 --> 00:10:24,781
Question six: how are records
without identifiers matched?

169
00:10:24,942 --> 00:10:28,115
By activity-log clusters
near the report time,

170
00:10:28,135 --> 00:10:33,941
matched by result, by elimination, and
by a self-calibrated seconds offset.

171
00:10:34,033 --> 00:10:37,215
This is the part most likely to lose points.

172
00:10:37,826 --> 00:10:41,114
Question seven: what would you do next?

173
00:10:41,274 --> 00:10:44,400
Add more evidence-grounded
checks for those matches,

174
00:10:44,420 --> 00:10:46,674
and test the vision fallback.

175
00:10:47,157 --> 00:10:53,639
To sum up: 518 requests audited, 100
percent status accuracy on the samples,

176
00:10:53,659 --> 00:10:56,447
and under three cents of model cost.

177
00:10:56,608 --> 00:10:58,457
Don't trust the word complete.

178
00:10:58,477 --> 00:11:00,394
Check every copy.
